@giacomozucco https://t.co/J4Kg8SnInj

But more seriously I get the point.

I think shifting technologies affect the average border size. Broader central communication (fast transmission, slow settlement) might expand the typical border that people build. Other factors, including alternative monies or decentralized communications (ie settlement that is almost as fast as transmission), if prevalent enough, might shrink the average border size, like what people identify as their own people.

I think I would separate the practical from the idealistic here.

In the idealistic sense, I view myself as an outlier. I live part time in Egypt and part time in the U.S., despite hating travel (maybe that’s why I hate travel). And I get your point that certain cultural connections can make me more in someone’s tribe than just where I am from, definitely. I know people from around the world, and for all practical purposes for myself would prefer no borders. Everyone who I’m cool with globally is more “like me” in many cases than a random neighbor near me.

In the practical sense, which I’ve argued since Broken Money and before, the gap between transaction speed and settlement speed gives power to centralized ledgers, aka central banks and nation states. That gives them tremendous power along with, at times, culture. Something like Japan is probably more culturally unified than India, but nonetheless a centralized ledger goes a long way together with some semblance of culture, and then people build around it.

And the other practical sense is just speed. The rate at which we can move discordant groups long distances is huge now. We can take millions of people from one place and put them in another within a generation, which results in more local pushback than when things happen over many lifetimes. And I don’t think that can be culturally ignored. Some places or setups are more suited to being melting pots than others (eg Singapore on the small scale, US on the large scale), but I can’t help but see a mess when I look how it is applied in many places.

